 ## 2026-08-10
 
+### 2011 — libssh2 adapter bounds cancellation-ignoring injected work (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
+- ROOT CAUSE: The generic timeout helper selected the deadline result but then drained its structured task group, so a resolver, connector, host policy, credential provider, or upload source that ignored task cancellation could hold the public operation and transport close beyond their configured deadlines.
+- REWORK: Timeout races now detach callers promptly while a bounded registry retains, cancels, and reports unfinished injected work until it actually returns. Upload keeps the libssh2 channel state machine actor-owned and applies one absolute deadline across open, source read, bounded drains, writes, EOF, and exit; only the injected source read is detached. External signer work follows the same visible ownership rule instead of making teardown wait indefinitely.
+- CONFORMANCE: Deterministic cancellation-ignoring resolver, upload-source, and signer fixtures prove operation/close return before release, retained work remains visible in the owned-resource snapshot, and explicit release restores tasks, channels, sockets, sessions, custom allocations, and buffers to baseline.
+- VERIFICATION: `swift test --filter LibSSH2` passes 37 tests/3 suites; `make validate-core` passes 371 tests/31 suites plus build and boundary checks; `make validate-libssh2`, source tamper gates, and the dual-architecture extension-safe macOS provider/harness matrix exit 0.
+
 ### 1943 — libssh2 adapter closes review round 5 fatal teardown and ownership gaps (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
 - ROOT CAUSE: Fatal socket failures were mapped inconsistently and could leave an apparently usable transport; automatic keepalive used its reply deadline while waiting behind KEX; teardown queues and task snapshots could grow or report optimistic zeros; and one-shot channel cleanup could discard a live libssh2 pointer after `EAGAIN`.
 - REWORK: One privacy-safe failure path now tears down every lane-fatal socket error. Automatic keepalive defers behind an active rekey and starts a fresh reply deadline after gate admission. Session/channel admission is capped, shared drains and teardown use single-flight tasks, and teardown cancels and joins all owned work before clearing handles. Channel close/free remains tracked until libssh2 confirms disposal; cleanup failure retains ownership for session teardown.
